In fact, Co. Kildare is known for its many culture events, whether it’s a theatre or musical performance in the Riverbank Arts Centre or one of the many annual festivals or events. There’s lots to see and do – why not combine your visit with a great event too?
The Emerald Isle has long been a stronghold of world class golf courses and with Kildare’s picturesque, undulating landscape lending itself perfectly to the sport it’s no wonder the county now boasts more than twenty courses, earning it the title of ‘Ireland’s golfing capital’. Kildare is famous for its championship golf courses scattered across the county.
The O’Meara Course, designed by two-time major winner Mark O’Meara, is a classic parkland course, weaving its way through enchanted woodlands while hugging the banks of the River Rye. By contrast, the Montgomerie Course pays homage to the best links courses from around the world.
A stone’s throw away, is the world-renowned 5-starK Clubin Straffan which hosted the Europe-winning Ryder Cup in 2006 and 13 European Opens. A visit to this venue is a must for golfers with the Arnold Palmer Ryder Cup Course and the Smurfit Course considered two of the best championship courses in Europe.
In constructing this championship course, the designers have cleverly used the River Greese as a natural hazard flowing through the Castle grounds and estate.
Two lakes have also been included in the course design, adding to the overall difficulty of the challenge of this royal and ancient game.
