Outdoor Enthusiasts, Things To Do
Kildare has some of Ireland’s finest gardens, all in blossom now.
We’ve weeded out some of the best options.
Burtown House in an early Georgian villa, surrounded by lush flower, vegetable and woodland gardens with park and farmland walks. Large herbaceous borders, shrubberies and even a large woodland garden island can all be found here and you’re in luck – the gardens come to life from February but May and June are peak months for variety and colour.
If you like flowers, you’re not going to be disappointed here where you’ll find a collection of trees and shrubs which won’t be found anywhere else in Ireland The site attracts worldwide gardening groups and dendrologists to examine the 15 acre garden. Many plan their visit in May to witness a most spectacular display of colour from azaleas and rhododendrons.
Situated in the Irish National Stud, the gardens were created to feed the soul as well as nurture the senses. The gardens were laid out over a four-year period, from 1906, by Japanese master horticulturist, Tassa Eida, to symbolize life though plants and water and rocks.
The parklands in Castletown House and Parklands are a must see with almost one million visitors a year. The recently restored eighteenth-century designed parklands and river walks are open every day throughout the year with no admission fee.
Check out the local businesses in Kildare and see the quality service that is offered right on your doorstep.
A unique cookery experience for all ages and abilities in this family-run Kilcullen cookery school.
Nolans Butchers was established in 1886 and was set up on the main street of a small village in Co.Kildare known as Kilcullen by the Nolan brothers. Newbridge
The national training academy for the Irish horseracing industry offering courses for jockeys, stable staff, racehorse trainers, breeders and others involved in the thoroughbred sector.
So, Firecastle opened in September 2020, as a brand-new build in Market Square, Kildare Town. It’s comprised of a few different elements – a café, a delicatessen, a bakery, and we’ve also got 10 bedrooms upstairs with their own private entrance… so it’s a bit of a hybrid of sorts.
The shelves in our grocery are packed with artisan goods, predominantly Irish produce. We stock everything… jams, milks, even cured meats, and we try to stock as much local Kildare produce as we can find.
Something that makes us unique to other stores is the fact that everything from our deli, bakery, and fresh range is made in-store by our dedicated team in the kitchen. We’ve actually got about 10-12 chefs working each day, and they create really delicious food that we’re very proud of!
Hmm… that’s a very good question, other than hit the golf course?
Taste of Kildare was set up by Into Kildare about 5 or 6 years ago, but we haven’t had the opportunity to do one since 2018 in the K Club, which was a fantastic event!
It is planned as a family fun and interactive event and will be a chance to gauge what people really feel about heritage and for them to feedback on what mediums work.
Located along the Grand Canal in Sallins, Lock13 brew their own hand-crafted excellent beers matched with quality food sourced locally from unbelievable suppliers.
So pack the suitcases, as ‘Into Kildare’ has put together a list of our top places to visit for the ultimate ‘girls trip’, which offers something for everyone in the group, from jewellery lovers, foodies, racing enthusiasts, culture vultures or shopping addicts, Kildare has you covered!
Kildare Village is a relaxed shopping haven, where spacious boutiques showcase the world’s leading fashion and lifestyle brands at discount prices. Kildare shopping village also has a range of restaurants, cafes and outdoor eating areas for you and the girls to make a day of it!
Start your culinary afternoon with one of their famous aperitifs or cocktails, which are sure to have a story as the owners love of foraging has allowed them create a range of their own juices and ferments to tempt you with!!
If you and the girls fancy a relaxing boat trip after all that racing and shopping, Athy Boat Tours provide private boat tours for up to six passengers on board “Freedom on the Water”. Whether you wish to enjoy a picnic on board during your tour to Levistown or lunch on the river bank during a day long trip to Maganey, a memorable experience is ensured.
The rooms are beautifully decorated with an amazing view of St. Brigid’s Cathedral and Round Tower. For brunch the next morning, why not check out Hartes who have the most delicious brunch menu which is available on weekends and not the mention their fantastic cocktails!
One of the best in Kildare, Killsahee Spa has some great packages that will have you spoilt for choice. Take some time out to relax, unwind and recharge with your pals in this luxurious setting and treat yourself to a well deserved spa treatment.
The trail begins where two rivers meet, the Liffey and the Rye, providing stunning views that set the scene for this enchanting journey. Leixlip was where Arthur’s vision became a reality – he surveyed the surrounding lands and deemed them perfect for his brewery, he received some of the start-up money for his venture from Archbishop Price who is buried in the nearby medieval church St Mary’s and he had the waterways to tow his product to the masses.
Your last stop on the trail will take you to Oughterard Cemetery, the final resting place of Arthur Guinness and some of his family members. The graveyard is set atop a small hill and gives awe-inspiring views of Kildare’s sweeping landscape and to the South the Dublin and Wicklow mountains.
