Unique heritage, stunning scenery, quality accommodation, spectacular cuisine and a world class shopping are just some of the experiences that await you with a warm welcome in our many towns and villages. For a day trip, or weekend away, enjoy the accessibility from all parts of Ireland.
Discover the rich history and heritage of this charming Liffeyside village. Explore the story of Arthur Guinness, walk the tranquil canal banks and visit some of the ‘Big Houses’ of Georgian Ireland.
Kildare is rich in culture, heritage, shopping and attractions. Spend a day at the races at the world-famous Curragh Racecourse, snap up designer deals in our shopping outlets and foodies will delight over the array of award-winning restaurants and gastro-pubs.
The historic town of Maynooth is Ireland’s only university town and a vibrant hub full of walks, cafés, eateries and things to do. It is bookended by Maynooth Castle at one end of the town, and 17th century Carton House at the other.
As the largest town in Kildare, Newbridge has a lot to offer. Take in a show at the Riverbank Arts Centre, pick up a special trinket in the famous Newbridge Silverware or take in a hard fought GAA match.
The warm and welcoming atmosphere in The Gallops of Kildare House Hotel makes it the perfect spot for a relaxing brunch
Experience delicious seasonal food at Kildare Village, with a menu to suit every taste.
The Silken Thomas not only have a fabulous breakfast menu but also a healthy options menu which includes yummy fruit & yogurt bowls, comforting bowls of porridge and delicious fresh baked scones. Did we forget to mention the smashed avocado and sourdough bread?
Located in the Irish National Stud & Gardens, the Japanese Gardens Restaurant is open from 9am and takes pride in offering simple, wholesome food with the emphasis on freshness and flavour.
The emphasis at Shoda Market Café is on good quality wholesome food, artisan coffee and a unique wine offering. Our favourite dish has to be the pancakes with fresh berries, fruit compote and Nutella.
