Leaves are changing, there is a chill in the air and the hats and scarves are emerging from storage. That can only mean one thing: autumn has officially landed.
Whether you’re looking for a short Sunday stroll, or an adventure through the woods, Kildare is blessed with it all. From lake loops and river walks, to forest hikes and historical trails, there is something for everybody to enjoy this October.
For a short afternoon stroll, follow the Lake Walk, which loops around waterlily-filled lake and takes no more than half an hour. The Nature Trail is just under 2km, which winds its way through some of the dramatic architecture of the estate.
This circular route can be walked in either direction and is great for walking furry friends, pushing strollers, or simply to get out for 30-minutes to enjoy the October sunshine.
The Riverside Trail
Enjoy an autumnal stroll, a family day out or even a tranquil weekend along the banks of one of Ireland’s most historical rivers, the River Barrow. With something of interest at every turn on this 200-year-old towpath, this river is the perfect companion for anyone walking or cycling along the Barrow Way.
This beautiful trail combines both the best of Ireland’s nature as well as its unique ancient history. Stretching from Castledermot to Oughterard near Straffan, this 92km trail will lead you to atmospheric ruins of age-old monasteries, relic round towers and time-worn rustic high crosses.
Just a five-minute drive from Rathangan Village lies the beautiful and relatively undiscovered Killinthomas Woods. Filled with bluebells in the spring and and orange floor of foliage in the autumn, there are options for both short and long walks, all starting and ending in the carpark.
My particular event is the Legends Museum, because that’s been my business all my life, racehorses, and as a new venture this is has proved very, very popular with the tourists that come, the racecourse that comes and the locals.
I’m available everyday in the museum and in every display cabinet we have there’s a story. There’s a story of a trainer or jockey and anybody that’s interested in the details other than just looking at the memorabilia, I give them the story or history of that particular person and the part they’ve played in Irish racing and the part they’ve played in Kildare Town.
Well, I could say Sea Biscuit, who was a famous horse in America in the 30’s, during the depression. He had crooked legs, he was small, he was given up as a bad job, and yet he won very, very many races including derbies in America and gave the people a focus on something they could forget about their troubles during the depression.
You will not see some of the things we have in the museum in the modern world, they were part of another era. Another good, very, very good era, that probably due to the lack of we’ll say media, television, and radio, didn’t get to the people the way it does now.
Well, I’d say at my age that’s definitely one of my mottos.
